<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-triple-integrals-spherical" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Triple Integrals in Spherical Coordinates</title>

<p>
Today, we will study yet another useful coordinate system in three
dimensions, which is known as the <em>spherical coordinate system</em>.
It simplifies the evaluation of triple integrals over regions bounded
by spheres or cones.
</p>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-spherical-coordinates">
<title>Spherical Coordinates</title>

<p>
The spherical coordinates can be best explained in a figure. Consider
the point <m>P</m> with spherical coordinates
<m>(\rho,\theta,\phi)</m> shown in
<xref ref="fig-spherical-point-tikz"/>.
</p>

<p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>
<m>\rho</m> is the distance of the point <m>P</m> from the origin
(<m>\rho \ge 0</m>).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\theta</m> is the angle the projection of
<m>\overrightarrow{OP}</m> makes with the positive <m>x</m>-axis. This
is the same <m>\theta</m> as in cylindrical coordinates,
<xref ref="eq-cyl-to-rect"/>
(<m>0 \le \theta \le 2\pi</m>).
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\phi</m> is the angle between <m>\overrightarrow{OP}</m> and the
positive <m>z</m>-axis (<m>0 \le \phi \le \pi</m>).
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-spherical-point-tikz">
<caption>
A point <m>P(\rho,\theta,\phi)</m> in spherical coordinates:
<m>\rho</m> is the distance from the origin, <m>\theta</m> is the
usual polar angle of the projection into the <m>xy</m>-plane, and
<m>\phi</m> is the angle between <m>\overrightarrow{OP}</m> and the
positive <m>z</m>-axis.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
A point in space located by a distance from the origin and two angles.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space. A segment of length rho runs from the
origin to a point P above the x y plane. The angle phi is marked
between the positive z axis and the segment, and the angle theta is
marked in the x y plane between the positive x axis and the dashed
projection of the segment. A dashed vertical line connects P to its
projection.
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// A point P(rho, theta, phi) in spherical coordinates.
// P = (1.15, 1.2, 1.5): theta0 = atan(1.2/1.15) = 46.2 deg, phi0 = acos(1.5/|OP|) = 47.9 deg
import graph3;

size(11cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={125}{20}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.7698, 0.539, 0.342));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);

triple P = (1.15, 1.2, 1.5);
triple Pxy = (P.x, P.y, 0);
real theta0 = atan2(P.y, P.x);
real phi0 = acos(P.z/abs(P));

// axes
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.8pt);
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), O--1.6X,  axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), O--1.75Y, axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), O--1.95Z, axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the planes of the two angles, lightly shaded
triple thetapt(pair t) { return t.y*(cos(t.x), sin(t.x), 0); }
triple phipt(pair t) {
  return t.y*(sin(t.x)*cos(theta0), sin(t.x)*sin(theta0), cos(t.x));
}
draw(surface(thetapt, (0, 0), (theta0, 0.62), 8, 1, Spline),
     0.3*oipurple + 0.7*white + opacity(0.5), light=nolight);
draw(surface(phipt, (0, 0), (phi0, 1.25), 8, 1, Spline),
     0.25*oiblue + 0.75*white + opacity(0.5), light=nolight);

// projection
pen dashpen = gray(0.4) + linewidth(0.9pt) + dashed;
draw(O--Pxy, dashpen);
draw(Pxy--P, dashpen);

// angle theta in the xy-plane: an arc of radius 0.62 from the x-axis to the projection
draw(arc(O, 0.62X, 0.62*unit(Pxy)), oipurple + linewidth(1.3pt), Arrow3(size=7));
// angle phi from the z-axis: an arc of radius 1.25 from the z-axis to the segment
draw(arc(O, 1.25Z, 1.25*unit(P)), oiblue + linewidth(1.3pt), Arrow3(size=7));

// rho segment and the point
draw(O--P, oivermillion + linewidth(1.8pt));
dot(P, oivermillion + linewidth(6pt));

// labels
label("$P(\rho,\theta,\phi)$", P, 2NE, oivermillion + fontsize(20pt));
label("$\rho$", 0.4*P, 1.5E, oivermillion + fontsize(20pt));
label("$\theta$", 0.9*(cos(theta0/2), sin(theta0/2), 0), oipurple + fontsize(20pt));
label("$\phi$", 0.88*(sin(phi0/2)*cos(theta0), sin(phi0/2)*sin(theta0), cos(phi0/2)),
      oiblue + fontsize(20pt));
label("$O$", O, NW, fontsize(20pt));

// keep the z label inside the frame
dot((0, 0, 2.2), invisible);
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-spherical-equations">
<title>Equations Relating Spherical Coordinates to Cartesian and Cylindrical Coordinates</title>
<p>
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-spherical-to-rect" number="yes">
r \amp = \rho\sin\phi,
</mrow>
<mrow>
z \amp = \rho\cos\phi,
</mrow>
<mrow>
x \amp = r\cos\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \rho\sin\phi\cos\theta,
</mrow>
<mrow>
y \amp = r\sin\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \rho\sin\phi\sin\theta,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\rho \amp = \sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \sqrt{r^2 + z^2}.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<p>
Spheres and cones have particularly simple equations in spherical
coordinates. For a sphere centered at the origin with radius <m>R</m>,
we have
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\amp x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = R^2
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \Rightarrow (\rho\sin\phi\cos\theta)^2 + (\rho\sin\phi\sin\theta)^2
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad + (\rho\cos\phi)^2 = R^2
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \Rightarrow \rho^2\left(\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta\right)\sin^2\phi
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad + \rho^2\cos^2\phi = R^2
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \Rightarrow \rho^2\sin^2\phi + \rho^2\cos^2\phi = R^2
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \Rightarrow \rho^2 = R^2 \Rightarrow \rho = R.
</mrow>
</md>

<example xml:id="example-spherical-shifted-sphere">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the equation of the sphere <m>x^2 + y^2 + (z-1)^2 = 1</m> in
spherical coordinates.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
Substituting the spherical expressions
<xref ref="eq-spherical-to-rect"/> for <m>x</m>, <m>y</m>, and
<m>z</m>:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\amp x^2 + y^2 + (z-1)^2 = 1
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \Rightarrow (\rho\sin\phi\cos\theta)^2 + (\rho\sin\phi\sin\theta)^2
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad + (\rho\cos\phi - 1)^2 = 1
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \Rightarrow \rho^2\left(\cos^2\theta + \sin^2\theta\right)\sin^2\phi
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad + \rho^2\cos^2\phi - 2\rho\cos\phi + 1 = 1
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \Rightarrow \rho^2\sin^2\phi + \rho^2\cos^2\phi
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad - 2\rho\cos\phi = 0
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \Rightarrow \rho^2 = 2\rho\cos\phi \Rightarrow \rho = 2\cos\phi.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<example xml:id="example-spherical-cones">
<title>Example II</title>
<statement>
<p>
What are the equations of the two cones
<m>z = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> and <m>z = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> in spherical
coordinates?
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
The upper cone makes a <m>45^\circ</m> angle with the positive
<m>z</m>-axis, so every point on it has <m>\phi = \pi/4</m>; likewise
the lower cone is <m>\phi = 3\pi/4</m>. (Algebraically:
<m>\rho\cos\phi = \rho\sin\phi</m> forces <m>\tan\phi = 1</m>.)
</p>
</solution>

<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-conversion-formulas">
<title>Coordinate Conversion Formulas</title>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \text{Cylindrical to rectangular:}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad x = r\cos\theta, \quad y = r\sin\theta, \quad z = z
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \text{Spherical to rectangular:}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad x = \rho\sin\phi\cos\theta, \quad y = \rho\sin\phi\sin\theta,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad z = \rho\cos\phi
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \text{Spherical to cylindrical:}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad r = \rho\sin\phi, \quad z = \rho\cos\phi, \quad \theta = \theta
</mrow>
</md>
The corresponding formulas for <m>dV</m> in triple integrals (see
<xref ref="eq-dv-cylindrical"/> and <xref ref="eq-dv-spherical"/>):
<md>
<mrow>
dV \amp = dx\,dy\,dz = dz\ r\,dr\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \rho^2\sin\phi\ d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-spherical-integration">
<title>Integration in Spherical Coordinates</title>

<p>
The volume element <m>dV</m> in spherical coordinates is illustrated
in <xref ref="fig-spherical-element-tikz"/>: a spherical wedge with
radial side <m>d\rho</m>, one arc side <m>\rho\,d\phi</m>, and the
other arc side <m>\rho\sin\phi\,d\theta</m>, so
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-dv-spherical">
dV = \rho^2\sin\phi\ d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta.
</men>

<figure xml:id="fig-spherical-element-tikz">
<caption>
The spherical volume element (spherical wedge), with sliders for the
three increments. Its three mutually perpendicular sides have lengths
<m>d\rho</m>, <m>\rho\,d\phi</m>, and <m>\rho\sin\phi\,d\theta</m>,
so <m>dV = \rho^2\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta</m>.
</caption>
<interactive geogebra="tkybvufd"
             width="100%" aspect="600:500" design-width="600"
             material-width="600" material-height="500"
             shift-drag-zoom="yes" reset-icon="yes">
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the background to rotate the picture, and use the three sliders to
change <m>d\rho</m>, <m>d\theta</m> and <m>d\phi</m>. None of the six
faces of the wedge is flat: two are pieces of spheres, two are pieces of
cones, and two are flat only because they are pieces of planes through
the <m>z</m>-axis.
</p>
<p>
Now pull the three sliders down toward zero. As the increments become
very small the curvature of those faces stops mattering, and the wedge
can be thought of as a box with edges <m>d\rho</m>,
<m>\rho\,d\phi</m> and <m>\rho\sin\phi\,d\theta</m>. Multiplying
those three edge lengths is exactly where
<m>dV = \rho^2\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta</m> comes from.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<p>
Then, the triple integral over a region <m>D</m> described in
spherical coordinates by
<m>\alpha\le\theta\le\beta</m>,
<m>\phi_{\min}\le\phi\le\phi_{\max}</m>, and
<m>g_1(\phi,\theta)\le\rho\le g_2(\phi,\theta)</m> can be converted
into spherical coordinates as follows:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\amp \iiint_D f(x,y,z)\,dV
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = \int_{\alpha}^{\beta}\!\!\int_{\phi_{\min}}^{\phi_{\max}}\!\!\int_{g_1(\theta,\phi)}^{g_2(\theta,\phi)}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad f\big(\rho\sin\phi\cos\theta,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad \rho\sin\phi\sin\theta,\ \rho\cos\phi\big)
</mrow>
<mrow xml:id="eq-triple-spherical" number="yes">
\amp \qquad \rho^2\sin\phi\ d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta.
</mrow>
</md>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-spherical-icecream">
<title>The Ice Cream Cone</title>

<example xml:id="example-spherical-icecream">
<title>Example III</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the volume of the <q>ice cream cone</q> <m>D</m> cut from the
solid sphere <m>\rho \le 1</m> by the cone <m>\phi = \pi/3</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
We begin by plotting the ice cream cone
(<xref ref="fig-icecream-tikz"/>). Next, we identify the region of
integration, which will provide us with the integration limits:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
D = \{(\rho,\theta,\phi) : \amp 0\le\theta\le 2\pi,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp 0\le\phi\le\frac{\pi}{3},
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp 0\le\rho\le 1\}.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Thus, by <xref ref="eq-volume-triple"/> and
<xref ref="eq-triple-spherical"/>, the volume of the ice cream cone can
be evaluated as follows:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
V \amp= \iiint_D \rho^2\sin\phi\ d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/3}\!\!\int_0^1
\rho^2\sin\phi\ d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/3}
\left[\frac{\rho^3}{3}\right]_0^1 \sin\phi\ d\phi\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/3} \frac13\sin\phi\ d\phi\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\left[-\frac13\cos\phi\right]_0^{\pi/3} d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^{2\pi}\left(-\frac16 + \frac13\right)d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \frac16(2\pi) = \frac{\pi}{3}.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-icecream-tikz">
<caption>
The <q>ice cream cone</q> of
<xref ref="example-spherical-icecream"/>: the region inside the
sphere <m>\rho = 1</m> and above the cone <m>\phi = \pi/3</m>. Its
volume is <m>\frac{\pi}{3}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="60%">
<shortdescription>
An ice cream cone shape: a cone from the origin capped by a piece of a sphere.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with an ice cream cone shaped solid: a
cone with vertex at the origin opening upward at sixty degrees from
the z axis, capped by a dome which is part of the unit sphere. The
circle where the cone meets the sphere is drawn.
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The "ice cream cone" D: inside the sphere rho = 1, above the cone phi = pi/3.
import graph3;

size(11cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={125}{18}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.779, 0.545, 0.309));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette, as in the TikZ figures
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);

real phi0 = pi/3;

// cone phi = pi/3, rho from 0 to 1 (param: theta, rho)
triple conept(pair t) {
  return t.y*(sin(phi0)*cos(t.x), sin(phi0)*sin(t.x), cos(phi0));
}
// spherical cap rho = 1, phi from 0 to pi/3 (param: phi, theta, so that the
// normal points out of D)
triple cappt(pair t) {
  return (sin(t.x)*cos(t.y), sin(t.x)*sin(t.y), cos(t.x));
}

surface Scone = surface(conept, (0, 0), (2pi, 1), 16, 1, Spline);
surface Scap  = surface(cappt, (0, 0), (phi0, 2pi), 6, 24, Spline);

draw(Scone, surfacepen=material(0.5*oigreen + 0.5*white + opacity(0.55),
                                emissivepen=0.3*oigreen, specularpen=gray(0.05)),
     meshpen=0.6*oigreen + linewidth(0.3pt) + opacity(0.35));
draw(Scap,  surfacepen=material(0.5*oiblue + 0.5*white + opacity(0.5),
                                emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)),
     meshpen=gray(0.3) + linewidth(0.3pt) + opacity(0.35));

// rim: where the cone meets the sphere (radius sin(pi/3), height cos(pi/3))
draw(circle((0, 0, cos(phi0)), sin(phi0)), oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt));

// axes; the parts inside D are seen through its translucent surfaces
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), (-1.15, 0, 0)--(1.35, 0, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), (0, -1.15, 0)--(0, 1.45, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), O--1.5Z,  axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// labels
label("Sphere $\rho = 1$", (0, 0.9, 0.86), E, oiblue + fontsize(17pt));
label("Cone $\phi = \frac{\pi}{3}$", (0, 0.75, 0.25), E,
      oigreen*0.6 + fontsize(17pt));
label("$D$", (0.55, -0.15, 0.62), fontsize(20pt));

// keep the axis labels inside the frame
dot((1.55, 0, 0), invisible); dot((0, 1.65, 0), invisible);
dot((0, 0, 1.75), invisible);
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The limits of integration can be read as three nested sweeps, and
<xref ref="fig-icecream-limits-manim"/> builds <m>D</m> from them,
innermost first.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-icecream-limits-manim">
<caption>
The limits of integration of <xref ref="example-spherical-icecream"/>,
animated: the ice cream cone built the way the iterated integral
<m>\int_0^{2\pi}\int_0^{\pi/3}\int_0^1 \rho^2\sin\phi\ d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta</m>
sweeps it. A ray at fixed <m>\theta</m> and <m>\phi</m> runs from
<m>\rho = 0</m> at the origin to <m>\rho = 1</m> on the sphere (yellow);
tilting it from <m>\phi = 0</m> on the <m>z</m>-axis to
<m>\phi = \pi/3</m> on the cone fills a flat fan (red); and turning the
fan from <m>\theta = 0</m> to <m>\theta = 2\pi</m> about the
<m>z</m>-axis sweeps out the solid (blue).
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-icecream-limits-manim"
             iframe="icecream-limits-manim.html"
             width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
The <m>\rho</m>, <m>\phi</m> and <m>\theta</m> sliders run the three
sweeps: the solid is drawn for the values of <m>\theta</m> already swept,
the fan at the current <m>\theta</m> for the values of <m>\phi</m>
already swept, and the ray at the current <m>\theta</m> and <m>\phi</m>
out to the current <m>\rho</m>. The card shows the integral with each
pair of limits in its sweep's color. <q>Surfaces</q> shows the sphere
and the cone that bound the solid, <q>Angles</q> marks <m>\phi</m> and
<m>\theta</m>, <q>Play</q> runs a guided tour, <q>Spin</q> turns the
figure (dragging turns it by hand), and <q>Full screen</q> fills the
screen with it.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<p>
How the spherical description of the ice cream cone
works<mdash/>the angle <m>\phi</m> sweeping from the <m>z</m>-axis
down to the cone while <m>\rho</m> reaches out to the
sphere<mdash/>is animated in <xref ref="fig-spherical-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-spherical-video">
<caption>
Animation of spherical coordinates and the ice cream cone. A ray from
the origin at angles <m>(\theta,\phi)</m> carries the point
<m>P(\rho,\theta,\phi)</m>; sweeping <m>\theta</m> spins the ray
around the <m>z</m>-axis, sweeping <m>\phi</m> tilts it away from the
axis, and restricting <m>0\le\rho\le 1</m>,
<m>0\le\phi\le\pi/3</m> fills out the ice cream cone of
<xref ref="example-spherical-icecream"/>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-spherical-icecream"
       source="videos/spherical_icecream_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-spherical-shifted-example">
<title>Converting a Triple Integral to Spherical Coordinates</title>

<example xml:id="example-spherical-convert">
<title>Example IV</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the triple integral below by converting it to spherical
coordinates:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\amp \int_{-2}^{2}\!\!\int_{-\sqrt{4-x^2}}^{\sqrt{4-x^2}}
\!\!\int_{2-\sqrt{4-x^2-y^2}}^{2+\sqrt{4-x^2-y^2}}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad \left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{3/2} dz\,dy\,dx.
</mrow>
</md>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
We begin by converting the integration limits and the integrand to
spherical coordinates. The limits describe the solid sphere
<m>x^2 + y^2 + (z-2)^2 = 4</m> of radius <m>2</m> centered at
<m>(0,0,2)</m>, shown in <xref ref="fig-shifted-sphere-tikz"/>:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\amp z = 2 \pm\sqrt{4-x^2-y^2}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \Rightarrow \rho\cos\phi = 2 \pm\sqrt{4 - \rho^2\sin^2\phi}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \Rightarrow (\rho\cos\phi - 2)^2 = 4 - \rho^2\sin^2\phi
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \Rightarrow \rho^2 = 4\rho\cos\phi \Rightarrow \rho = 4\cos\phi.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Note that <m>z = 2+\sqrt{4-x^2-y^2}</m> is the upper hemisphere and
<m>z = 2-\sqrt{4-x^2-y^2}</m> is the lower hemisphere. In spherical
coordinates the solid is <m>0\le\rho\le 4\cos\phi</m>,
<m>0\le\phi\le\frac{\pi}{2}</m>, <m>0\le\theta\le 2\pi</m>, and the
integrand is
<m>\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{3/2} = \left(\rho^2\right)^{3/2}
= \rho^3</m>. Thus <xref ref="eq-triple-spherical"/> gives:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\amp \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_0^{4\cos\phi}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad \left(\rho^2\right)^{3/2}\rho^2\sin\phi\ d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_0^{4\cos\phi}
\rho^5\sin\phi\ d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \frac{4^6}{6}\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/2}
\cos^6\phi\,\sin\phi\ d\phi\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = -\frac{4^6}{42}\int_0^{2\pi}
\Big[\cos^7\phi\Big]_0^{\pi/2}\,d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \frac{4096\pi}{21}.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-shifted-sphere-tikz">
<caption>
The sphere <m>x^2 + y^2 + (z-2)^2 = 4</m> of
<xref ref="example-spherical-convert"/>, which in spherical
coordinates is <m>\rho = 4\cos\phi</m>. It sits on the
<m>xy</m>-plane, touching the origin.
</caption>
<image width="65%">
<shortdescription>
A sphere resting on the origin, centered on the vertical axis.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a sphere of radius two centered on
the z axis at height two, so it touches the origin from above. A
dashed vertical diameter runs from the origin to the north pole at
height four.
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The sphere x^2 + y^2 + (z-2)^2 = 4, that is rho = 4 cos(phi).
import graph3;

size(10cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={125}{15}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.7912, 0.554, 0.2588));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);

// sphere rho = 4 cos(phi): center (0,0,2), radius 2 (param: phi, theta, so
// that the normal points outward)
triple spherept(pair t) {
  return (2*sin(t.x)*cos(t.y), 2*sin(t.x)*sin(t.y), 2 + 2*cos(t.x));
}
surface S = surface(spherept, (0, 0), (pi, 2pi), 12, 24, Spline);
draw(S, surfacepen=material(0.5*oiblue + 0.5*white + opacity(0.35),
                            emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)),
     meshpen=gray(0.3) + linewidth(0.3pt) + opacity(0.35));

// equator
draw(circle((0, 0, 2), 2), oiblue + linewidth(1.0pt));

// axes; the parts inside the sphere are seen through it
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), (-2.3, 0, 0)--(2.6, 0, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), (0, -2.3, 0)--(0, 2.7, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), O--(0, 0, 4.6), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// dashed vertical diameter
draw(O--(0, 0, 4), gray(0.4) + linewidth(0.8pt) + dashed);
dot(O, linewidth(4pt));
dot((0, 0, 2), linewidth(4pt));
dot((0, 0, 4), linewidth(4pt));

// labels
label("$x^2 + y^2 + (z-2)^2 = 4$", (0, 1.7, 4.55), E, fontsize(17pt));
label("$\rho = 4\cos\phi$", (0, 1.7, 4.05), E, oivermillion + fontsize(17pt));
label("$(0,0,4)$", (0, 0, 4), 1.5W + N, fontsize(14pt));
label("$(0,0,2)$", (0, 0, 2), E, fontsize(14pt));

// keep the z label inside the frame
dot((0, 0, 4.9), invisible);
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-spherical-rewrite">
<title>Re-Writing an Integral in Spherical and Cylindrical Coordinates</title>

<example xml:id="example-spherical-rewrite">
<title>Example V</title>
<statement>
<p>
Re-write (do not evaluate) the following integral in
<em>spherical</em> and <em>cylindrical</em> coordinates:
</p>

<me>
\int_{-1}^{1}\!\!\int_{-\sqrt{1-x^2}}^{0}
\!\!\int_{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}^{1}
(x^2+y^2)\, dz\,dy\,dx.
</me>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
The <m>xy</m>-projection is the lower half disk
<m>y \le 0</m>, <m>x^2+y^2\le 1</m> (shown in
<xref ref="fig-halfdisk-prefigure"/>), so
<m>\pi \le \theta \le 2\pi</m> and <m>0 \le r \le 1</m>. In
<m>z</m>, the solid runs from the cone <m>z = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>
(that is, <m>z = r</m>) up to the plane <m>z = 1</m>; the solid is
shown in <xref ref="fig-cone-plane-tikz"/>. Since
<m>x^2 + y^2 = r^2</m>, the cylindrical form
<xref ref="eq-triple-cylindrical"/> is
</p>

<me>
\int_{\pi}^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^1\!\!\int_r^1 r^2\, r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta.
</me>

<p>
For the spherical form, the cone is <m>\phi = \pi/4</m>, and the
plane <m>z = 1</m> becomes <m>\rho\cos\phi = 1</m>, that is,
<m>\rho = \sec\phi</m>. With
<m>x^2 + y^2 = r^2 = \rho^2\sin^2\phi</m>,
<xref ref="eq-triple-spherical"/> gives
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\amp \int_{\pi}^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/4}\!\!\int_0^{\sec\phi}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad \left(\rho^2\sin^2\phi\right)\rho^2\sin\phi\ d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta.
</mrow>
</md>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-halfdisk-prefigure">
<caption>
The <m>xy</m>-projection of the solid in
<xref ref="example-spherical-rewrite"/>: the lower half of the unit
disk, described by <m>\pi\le\theta\le 2\pi</m>, <m>0\le r\le 1</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<shortdescription>
The lower half of the unit disk, shaded.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-halfdisk">
<diagram dimensions="(405,215)" margins="10">
<definition>arc(t) = (cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-1.35,2.3,0.65)">
<axes at="hd-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<polygon at="hd-region" fill="#f5c6bc" stroke="none" closed="yes"
    points="((1,0),(0.9877,-0.1564),(0.9511,-0.309),(0.891,-0.454),(0.809,-0.5878),(0.7071,-0.7071),(0.5878,-0.809),(0.454,-0.891),(0.309,-0.9511),(0.1564,-0.9877),(0,-1),(-0.1564,-0.9877),(-0.309,-0.9511),(-0.454,-0.891),(-0.5878,-0.809),(-0.7071,-0.7071),(-0.809,-0.5878),(-0.891,-0.454),(-0.9511,-0.309),(-0.9877,-0.1564),(-1,0))"/>
<parametric-curve at="hd-arc" function="arc" domain="(pi,2*pi)"
    stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<line at="hd-diam" endpoints="((-1,0),(1,0))" stroke="#555555"
    thickness="2"/>
<label at="hd-eq" anchor="(0.85,-0.53)" alignment="se"
    color="#D55E00"><m>y = -\sqrt{1-x^2}</m></label>
<point at="hd-1" p="(1,0)" fill="black" alignment="ne">
<m>1</m>
</point>
<point at="hd-m1" p="(-1,0)" fill="black" alignment="nw">
<m>-1</m>
</point>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The lower half of the unit disk, below the x axis, shaded light red. Its curved boundary y equals negative square root of one minus x squared is drawn in vermillion.">
  <annotation ref="hd-region" text="The projection: the lower half of the unit disk, where theta runs from pi to two pi."/>
  <annotation ref="hd-arc" text="The lower half of the unit circle."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-cone-plane-tikz">
<caption>
The solid of <xref ref="example-spherical-rewrite"/>: over the lower
half disk, between the cone <m>z = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> (spherical
<m>\phi = \pi/4</m>) and the plane <m>z = 1</m> (spherical
<m>\rho = \sec\phi</m>).
</caption>
<image width="75%">
<shortdescription>
A half cone over the negative y half plane, capped by a flat half disk.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a half cone opening upward from
the origin at forty five degrees, over the half of the plane with y
negative, capped above by the flat half disk in the plane z equals
one. The straight edge of the cap lies over the x axis.
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The solid over the lower half disk, between the cone z = sqrt(x^2+y^2)
// (phi = pi/4) and the plane z = 1 (rho = sec(phi)).
import graph3;

size(11cm);
// Same viewpoint as the pgfplots figure (view={35}{18}).
currentprojection = orthographic((0.5455, -0.7791, 0.309));
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);

// half cone z = r over theta in [pi, 2pi] (param: theta, r)
triple conept(pair t) { return t.y*(cos(t.x), sin(t.x), 1); }
// top half disk z = 1 (param: r, theta, so that the normal points up)
triple diskpt(pair t) { return (t.x*cos(t.y), t.x*sin(t.y), 1); }

draw(surface(conept, (pi, 0), (2pi, 1), 12, 1, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.5*oigreen + 0.5*white + opacity(0.5),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oigreen, specularpen=gray(0.05)),
     meshpen=0.6*oigreen + linewidth(0.3pt) + opacity(0.35));
draw(surface(diskpt, (0, pi), (1, 2pi), 1, 12, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.5*oiblue + 0.5*white + opacity(0.45),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));

// boundary of the top half disk
path3 halfcircle(real h) { return arc((0, 0, h), (1, 0, h), (-1, 0, h), -Z); }
draw(halfcircle(1)--cycle, oiblue + linewidth(1.2pt));

// cone edge lines
draw(O--(1, 0, 1), 0.6*oigreen + linewidth(1.0pt));
draw(O--(-1, 0, 1), 0.6*oigreen + linewidth(1.0pt));

// projection half disk (dashed)
draw(halfcircle(0)--cycle, oivermillion + linewidth(1.0pt) + dashed);

// axes
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), (-1.3, 0, 0)--(1.5, 0, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), (0, -1.5, 0)--(0, 1.5, 0), axispen,
     Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(20pt)), O--(0, 0, 1.6), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// labels
dot((-1.75, 0.6, 0.5), invisible);  // room for the cone label
dot((0, 0, 1.85), invisible);       // and for the z label
label("$z = 1$", (1, 0, 1), 2E, oiblue + fontsize(17pt));
label("$z = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}$", (-0.41, -0.285, 0.5), 3W, 0.6*oigreen + fontsize(17pt));
label("projection", (0.578, -0.816, 0), 2S, oivermillion + fontsize(17pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-cone-plane-cyl-manim"/> and
<xref ref="fig-cone-plane-sph-manim"/> build the solid from each set of
limits in turn, innermost first: the two descriptions fill the same flat
triangle in different ways before turning it about the <m>z</m>-axis.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-cone-plane-cyl-manim">
<caption>
The cylindrical limits of <xref ref="example-spherical-rewrite"/>,
animated: the solid built the way
<m>\int_{\pi}^{2\pi}\int_0^1\int_r^1 r^2\, r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta</m>
sweeps it. A vertical segment at fixed <m>r</m> and <m>\theta</m> runs
from the cone <m>z = r</m> up to the plane <m>z = 1</m> (yellow); letting
<m>r</m> run from <m>0</m> to <m>1</m> fills a flat triangle (red); and
turning the triangle from <m>\theta = \pi</m> to <m>\theta = 2\pi</m>,
over the lower half disk, sweeps out the solid (blue).
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-cone-plane-cyl-manim"
             iframe="cone-plane-cyl-manim.html"
             width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
The <m>z</m>, <m>r</m> and <m>\theta</m> sliders run the three sweeps:
the solid is drawn for the values of <m>\theta</m> already swept, the
triangle at the current <m>\theta</m> for the values of <m>r</m> already
swept, and the segment at the current <m>r</m> and <m>\theta</m> up to
the current <m>z</m>. The card shows the integral with each pair of
limits in its sweep's color. <q>Surfaces</q> shows the cone, the plane
and the half disk below them, <q>Coordinates</q> marks <m>r</m> and
<m>\theta</m>, <q>Play</q> runs a guided tour, <q>Spin</q> turns the
figure (dragging turns it by hand), and <q>Full screen</q> fills the
screen with it.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-cone-plane-sph-manim">
<caption>
The spherical limits of <xref ref="example-spherical-rewrite"/>,
animated: the same solid built the way
<m>\int_{\pi}^{2\pi}\int_0^{\pi/4}\int_0^{\sec\phi}
\left(\rho^2\sin^2\phi\right)\rho^2\sin\phi\ d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta</m>
sweeps it. A ray at fixed <m>\theta</m> and <m>\phi</m> runs from the
origin to the plane <m>z = 1</m>, where <m>\rho = \sec\phi</m> (yellow);
letting <m>\phi</m> run from <m>0</m> to <m>\pi/4</m> fills the same
triangle (red); and turning it from <m>\theta = \pi</m> to
<m>\theta = 2\pi</m> sweeps out the solid (blue).
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="interactive-cone-plane-sph-manim"
             iframe="cone-plane-sph-manim.html"
             width="100%" aspect="1:1">
<instructions>
<p>
The <m>\rho</m>, <m>\phi</m> and <m>\theta</m> sliders run the three
sweeps: the solid is drawn for the values of <m>\theta</m> already
swept, the triangle at the current <m>\theta</m> for the values of
<m>\phi</m> already swept, and the ray at the current <m>\theta</m> and
<m>\phi</m> out to the current <m>\rho</m>. The card shows the integral
with each pair of limits in its sweep's color. <q>Surfaces</q> shows the
cone, the plane and the half disk below them, <q>Coordinates</q> marks
<m>\phi</m> and <m>\theta</m>, <q>Play</q> runs a guided tour,
<q>Spin</q> turns the figure (dragging turns it by hand), and <q>Full
screen</q> fills the screen with it.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-spherical-volume">
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the volume of the region inside <m>\rho = 2\cos\phi</m> and
below <m>z = \sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
<m>\dfrac{\pi}{3}</m>.
</p>
</answer>
</exercise>

</subsection>

</section>
